Byron A Jeff schrieb:
> 
> In article <1173209060.006099.23680@8g2000cwh.googlegroups.com>,
>  <masquerade808@gmail.com> wrote:
> >I've heard mentions in many different places of intentionally avoiding
> >fights and decreasing your own level as a strategy. This is a newbie
> >question, but what is the advantage of limiting or decreasing your
> >level, what classes should do it, and when?
> 
> I've been out of town otherwise I would have tackled this earlier.
> 
> At its ultimate level you're talking about pacifist conduct. It's absolutely
> one of the toughest conducts in the game.

And while i've never played one, for a pacifist, going on a fast dive
to minetown to buy protection sounds like a good idea. 

> Nethack gives characters lots of good attributes for experience. Here are a
> few:
> 
> - Hit points

One of the most significant benefits in the early game. And healers
have a way around that with their starting gear.

> - extra skill slots which can improve accuracy (and damage too I think?)

Yes; better damage for weapon and combat skills; i don't know whether
advancing the 'attack spells' skill improves spell damage. 

You didn't mention it, but your level is factored into the chance of
hitting things: +1 level = +1 to-hit. That's a significant factor for
weapon-users in the early game.

> Now to balance the game, there has to be a penalty. The penalty is that the
> RNG generates monsters whos level are around the average of your XL and your
> DL.

That's not a penalty - the game provides you with the opponents
required for you to keep levelling up. If nowt but newts and grid bugs
were generated ever, you'd have a hard time getting into the quest
(well, you wouldn't reach level 6, because extincting newts and grid
bugs gives 240 xp, while reaching level 6 requires 320).

> Finally once you have the Amulet
> (or kill Rodney I can't remember which) the DL is maxed out to the bottom of
> the dungeon for the rest of the game no matter what XL you are.

The condition is holding the amulet - when you drop the amulet,
monster generation will be based on the actual dungeonlevel again (if
you're adventurous, you can use Rodney or a demon lord to carry the
Amulet through the regular dungeon for you; make sure the bugger
hasn't got a wand of digging).

> I'm running monks right now and with protection, the given
> equipment (+1 robe, +2 gloves), and the typical dwarven helm and shoes that
> routinely drop in the upper mines, I can get my AC down in the -8 to -10
> range without too much of a problem. Typically I can function unburdened
> because I don't need to wear armor (real bad for monks anyway).

While playing pacifist-style, wearing armour can be a good choice for
a monk - with the starting gear, an ordinary chainmail puts you at AC
-1 _and_ prevents you from accidentally hitting things, too.

> Now post quest there's yet another debate. Typically any XL14 or XL15
> character can get access to magic/divine intervention that improves most
> of the gains that you get from increasing XL.

XL10 with maxed luck and fully enchanted gear is quite enough for a
melee-based character to beat the planes (you'll want to have over 100
HP, but that's easy enough to get with potions). It's different if
spells are a major concern, particularly when you do much of your
damage via magic - spell damage is mainly derived from experience
level.

Of course, past XL15, it's very hard to gain additional levels from
killing stuff: without gain-level effects, kills in a complete
ascension game usually amount to enough experience for level 16 or 17.
XL18 takes a fairly lengthy game, XL21+ may well be impossible to
achieve by kills outside the Astral Plane.

